Current original Zero workflow (0.12)

Use the native tools on Linux or their .exe versions on Windows:
  zero-integrate PROJECT_DIRECTORY NEW_KERNEL.zero
  zero-build PROJECT_DIRECTORY NEW_OUTPUT_DIRECTORY NEW_KERNEL.zero
  zero-check PROJECT_DIRECTORY NEW_TEST_DIRECTORY
All destination paths must be new. The integrator preserves authored sources;
the builder checks its compiler fixed point and builds the original targets;
the verifier checks language behavior and host file operations in process.
See tools/integrate.ztext, tools/build.ztext and checks/README.ztext.
Read these documents with zero-text read FILE.

The notes below preserve earlier implementation and test history. Any old
shell/Python runner names, generated artifacts, JSON reports or commands are
historical references: they were removed from the active project and are not
current runnable instructions or current-release validation. Native host
checks do not replace BIOS/UEFI, persistence or physical-hardware testing.

Historical implementation notes

ZERO WINDOW RENDERER

worker.zero.part is original Zero code integrated into the graphics worker.
It declares no additional language variables. It uses private thread-frame
locals and only the shared ABI and scratch ranges documented below.
No borrowed compositor, UI toolkit, font library or artwork is used.

Root-owned ABI at console offsets (u64)
2048 slot pool base;2056 capacity;2064 focused slot index;2072 open count;
2080 layout (0 tiles,1 scrolling);2088 layout generation.
Each slot is8192 bytes. Fields:0 alive,8 displayed ID,16 output-ring write
head,24 ring count,32 input length,40 overflow,48 reserved,
56 selected root,64 dirty generation,72 last rendered dirty generation.
Input storage starts256, output ring starts1024 and holds7168 bytes.
The renderer never changes input, ring contents or selected roots.
Only the captured value of dirty generation is committed to slot+72.
CR/LF input suppression is global device-stream state, not per-window state.

Graphics-owned shared scratch
console+2304..2315: formatted open-window count.
console+2336..2348: formatted terminal title.
console+2368: current caret phase;2376 phase changed;2384 committed phase.
console+2392: graphics UI scale (2 at exact1920x1080, otherwise1).
console+3072..7679: at most96 columns by48 rows of ASCII cells.
All layout, geometry, replay and glyph temporaries otherwise live in private
thread variables. No memory at console+8192 or above is touched by this code.

Display ownership and scheduling
With an active slot pool, the worker owns all framebuffer/VGA drawing. Root
appends output to its active ring and handles serial output independently.
Root must reset ring count/head on clear rather than append ANSI clear bytes
to the graphical log. Root does not schedule graphics while paused.
Each framebuffer row is bounded; the worker yields after rows or short text
pieces. The layout generation is checked before subsequent painting and
again before committing the rendered generation after the final yield.
Closing/reusing slots or changing focus/layout aborts the old frame; the next
iteration clears and paints the new layout. Ring replay itself never yields,
so it reads one stable snapshot of root-written head/count/content.
Root may append during drawing. The renderer commits the old dirty snapshot,
leaving that window dirty for the next iteration instead of losing updates.

Layouts
Tiles show up to3 live windows on the page containing the focused window.
Holes from closed slots are skipped. One window fills the workspace; two get
equal columns; three use a master window left and two stacked windows right.
Focus on another group automatically selects that page.
Scrolling shows the focused column centered, with the previous and next
live columns clipped at screen edges. It never wraps clipped text into a
neighbor. More open windows remain available through focus navigation.

At800x600 with3 windows:
  tile1 x16,y104,w440,h436
  tile2 x472,y104,w312,h210
  tile3 x472,y330,w312,h210
With2 windows: x16 and408, eachw376,h436,y104.
Scroll: column width368; focusedx216; previous virtualx-168; nextx600;
all y104,h436. Negative origins are represented as a nonnegative screen
origin plus an explicit source crop, avoiding unsigned pointer underflow.

At other supported sizes, topY is104 for height>=600, otherwise80. Full
window height is screenHeight-topY-60. Visible text begins atwindowX+16,
windowY+50. Columns=floor((windowWidth-32)/8), clamped96; rows=floor(
(windowHeight-76)/16), clamped48. Small stacked windows have fewer rows.
Header is34pixels plus divider, footer24pixels, border1pixel.
Body cells are8x16 using the kernel's original5x7 ASCII glyph rows doubled
vertically. Every pixel is clipped to its logical window and physical screen;
framebuffer stride and RGB/BGR ordering are honored.

Full HD rendering (0.8.1)
An actual1920x1080 framebuffer uses a960x540 logical layout with UI scale2.
The renderer keeps the physical framebuffer address and stride unchanged.
Every logical pixel is written directly as a2x2 group of physical pixels;
headers, title bars, body glyphs, clipping, borders and padding all use the
same scale. This is native framebuffer drawing, not screenshot enlargement.
Other resolutions, including800x600 and640x480/400, retain the1x layout.
No additional allocation or language variable is needed; the existing
private command_status local holds the scale. The text grid still fits the
same96x48 bound. The framebuffer size is owned/validated by the boot loader.

Exact physical geometry at1920x1080 with3 live tile windows:
  master x32,y160,w1040,h800
  second x1104,y160,w784,h384
  third x1104,y576,w784,h384
With2 windows: x32 and976, eachw912,h800,y160.
Scroll focused column: x512,y160,w896,h800. Previous virtualx-416 and next
x1440 are clipped at physical screen edges. Each body starts32 pixels from
its window's logical-left edge and100pixels below the top. Cells are16x32.
The master has61columns20rows; stacked windows45columns7rows; two equal
tiles53columns20rows. Header/footer placement derives from logical540height.
The per-yield layout checks, captured dirty-generation commits, shared caret
phase and serial/VGA fallbacks remain the same as before.

Output replay
Each byte-ring snapshot reflows to that window's current width. CR returns
to column0; LF starts a new line; backspace moves left or to the preceding
row; printable ASCII wraps at the right edge. A circular row index performs
vertical scrolling without copying the whole grid per line. Oldest bytes
beyond the7168-byte ring are discarded by root. No interactive scrollback
navigation or Unicode shaping is implemented here.
A focused window shows an underscore caret. animate off leaves it visible;
animate on follows the existing polled decisecond clock, alternating every
4 ticks. A phase change redraws only the focused window, not the background
or its neighbors. Phase commits also require a stable layout generation.

Theme and fallback
The default uses the preview's navy/lavender palette. Theme1 shifts the
background/panel colors to teal and uses cyan focus borders.
VGA text mode displays only the focused window: a title row plus24 rows of
80-column body text. Other sessions remain open and can receive focus.
Serial-only mode marks the renderer ready and yields without visual writes.
With no allocated pool, the renderer also marks ready and yields so root's
legacy fallback remains usable. The initial ready flag is set after a stable
complete render, not merely after entering the worker.

Isolated validation completed
- Embedded fragment compiled as full raw64 and raw32 kernel source.
- Hosted renderer checked800x600,640x480,640x400 with16 extra stride pixels
  per row,128-byte guards before and after the framebuffer. Every guard and
  row-padding byte retained its sentinel in tile and scroll cases.
- RGB and BGR renderings decode to byte-identical RGB output at all3 sizes.
- An independent terminal replay oracle passed9 cases: empty ring, CR/LF,
  backspace/erase sequence, exact-width wrapping, wrap to next row, CR
  overwrite, backspace at start, many-line scrolling and a wrapped full ring.
- Native800x600 tile and scrolling outputs were visually inspected.
These hosted checks isolate the renderer; integrated boot, keyboard routing,
window lifecycle and disk isolation are validated by the VM task separately.

Reproducible hosted check sources and orchestration are under
review-tests/wm-renderer/. Run bash review-tests/wm-renderer/run.sh.
Committed results, exact worker hash, decoded grids and six fixture PNGs
are under verification/wm-renderer-0.8.0/. Full generated binaries and PPMs
remain internal build artifacts. This suite deliberately removes yields;
no commit-generation abort/interleaving test is claimed for it.

Full HD hosted validation (0.8.1)
review-tests/wm-renderer-hd/run.sh tests the production worker at1920x1080,
960x540,800x600,640x480 and640x400. Every case checks128-byte framebuffer
guards,16physical stride-padding pixels per row and the text-grid end
sentinel. RGB/BGR decode identically. Both FullHD layouts match every pixel
of an independently rendered960x540 view expanded as native2x2 groups.
All1x compact outputs match the frozen0.8.0 worker byte for byte.
The own Zero test helper expands only hosted fixture arenas from4MiB to16MiB
and validates their ELF/prologue before changing the known size immediate.
The host fixture stack allowance is increased for this framebuffer test;
production compiler/kernel allocation limits are not changed by the helper.
Reports and fixture images: verification/wm-renderer-hd-0.8.1/.
Actual firmware mode selection and input tests are separate VM evidence.
